<resources>
    <!-- Habitat overlays of the map (slope orientation, tree species and soil). -->
    <string name="habitat_section_title">Habitat layers</string>
    <string name="habitat_section_description">Drawn on top of any base map. They need an internet connection: without it they simply stay empty. Pick the classes to show in the habitat legend.</string>
    <string name="habitat_aspect_title">Slope orientation</string>
    <string name="habitat_aspect_description">North-facing slopes (blue lines) are shadier and wetter, usually better for mushrooms; south-facing ones (orange lines) are sunnier and drier. Drawn as lines so the layers below stay visible. Computed from elevation data.</string>
    <string name="habitat_trees_title">Tree types</string>
    <string name="habitat_trees_description">Dominant tree species of every forest, from the Spanish Forest Map (MITECO). Visible from zoom level 7.</string>
    <string name="habitat_soil_title">Soil (substrate)</string>
    <string name="habitat_soil_description">Acid (siliceous) or basic (calcareous) bedrock, from the lithological map of the IGME. Many mushrooms prefer one of them: chanterelles and many boletes grow on acid soils, while others such as some milk caps or morels prefer limestone. Rough guide at 1:1,000,000.</string>

